'''''Lomelosia''''' is a [[genus]] of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Caprifoliaceae]] and the subfamily of	[[Dipsacoideae]].
The genus includes over 50-63, [[Perennial plant|perennial]] and [[Annual plant|annual]] species, diffused around the Mediterranean Sea, with the greatest diversity of species concentrated in the eastern Mediterranean and the Middle East (including the Arabian Peninsula), and also has a few species reaching as far east as China.<ref name="Powo"/>

Morphologically, the genus accommodates all ''Scabiosa'' taxa with a pitted epicalyx. (an [[involucre]] (structure surrounding or supporting, usually a head of flowers) resembling an outer calyx (collective term for the sepals of the flower).<ref name=Belgium/>

Some older books still list them under ''Scabiosa''.<ref>James Cullen, Sabina G. Knees, H. Suzanne Cubey and J. M. H. Shaw (Editors) {{google books|KLPcml_QiFQC|The European Garden Flora Flowering Plants: A Manual for the Identification ... (2011)|page=360}}</ref> or list both names together. Such as ''Scabiosa sicula'' {{au|L.}} [''Lomelosia divaricata'' {{au|Jacq.) Greuter & Burdet}}].<ref>Joseph W. Shaw and Maria C. Shaw (Editors) {{google books|6qL_AwAAQBAJ|Kommos: An Excavation on the South Coast of Crete, Volume I, Part I: The ... (2014)|page=140}}</ref>

==Taxonomy==
''Lomelosia'' was first published and described by french botanist [[Constantine Samuel Rafinesque|Rafinesque]] in Flora Telluriana Vol.4 on page 95 in 1838, with a general description of ''Lomelosia stellata'' {{au|(L.) Raf.}}<ref name="Powo"/>

The name ''Lomelosia'' is derived from the [[Greek language|Greek]] word ''Loma'' meaning 'the edge or the border' which refers to the membranous border of the flower.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Johnson |first1=A.T. |last2=Smith |first2=H.A. |last3=Stockdale |first3=A.P. |title=Plant names simplified : their pronunciation, derivation and meaning |date=1931 |publisher=W. H. & L. Collingridge |isbn=9781910455067 |page=90 |edition=3rd revised}}</ref>

===History===
[[File:Lomelosia caucasica 2021-10-09 01.jpg|thumb|right|Flower of ''Lomelosia caucasica'' in Wrocław University Botanical Garden, Wrocław, Poland]]
It was included in ''Scabiosa'' {{au|L.}} for quite a long time (e.g. Jasiewicz 1976),<ref>{{cite book |last1=Jasiewicz |first1=A. |editor1-last=Tutin |editor1-first=T.G. |editor2-last=Heywood |editor2-first=V.H. |editor3-last=Burges |editor3-first=N.A. |editor4-last=Valentine |editor4-first=D.H. |editor5-last=Walters |editor5-first=S.M. |editor6-last=Webb |editor6-first=D.A. |title=Flora Europea |date=1976 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |location=Cambridge |pages=68–74 |edition=vol. 4 |chapter=Scabiosa}}</ref>

Earlier researchers had considered that the annual species  of ''Scabiosa'' to be derived within ''Lomelosia'' and hypothesized that this was responsible for the successful colonization of the (mostly) Eastern Mediterranean, (Ehrendorfer, 1965a);<ref name="Ehrendorfer1965a">{{cite book |last1=Ehrendorfer |first1=F. |editor1-last=Baker |editor1-first=H.G. |editor2-last=Stebbins |editor2-first=J.L. |title=The Genetics of Colonizing Species. |date=1965 |publisher=Academic Press |location=New York, USA |pages=331–352 |chapter=Dispersal mechanisms, genetic systems, and colonizing abilities in some flowering plant families.}}</ref> and Verlaque, 1986b.<ref name="Verlaque1986b">{{cite journal |last1=Verlaque |first1=R. |title=Étude biosystématique et phylogénétique des Dipsacaceae. IV.— Tribus des Scabioseae (phylum #4) et conclusion. |journal=Rev. Cytol. Biol. Veg. Botaniste |date=1986 |volume=9 |pages=97–176}}</ref>

For very many years ''Lomelosia caucasica'' was known as ''Scabiosa caucasica'' {{au|M. Bieb.}} as well as several other species of ''Scabiosa''.<ref>S. K. Czerepanov {{google books|Up0XgqTk2pkC|Vascular Plants of Russia and Adjacent States (the Former USSR) (1995)|page=216 }}</ref> Then following a comprehensive study of the family [[Dipsacaceae]] by Verlaque in 1983,<ref>{{cite book |last1=Verlaque |first1=R. |title=Etude biogéographique et phylogénétique des Dipsacaceae 2. - Thèse |date=1983 |publisher=Academie Aix-Marseilles I |location=[[University of Provence|Université de Province]] (St Charles)}}</ref> and by Devesa in 1984,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Devesa |first1=J A. |title=Revision of the genus ''Scabiosa'' in Spain and Balearic Islands. |journal=Lagascalia |date=1984 |volume=12 |pages=143–212}}</ref> the Scabioseae subfamily were then split into several genera, with ''S.&nbsp;caucasica'' placed in ''Lomelosia'' {{au|Raf.}} (Greuter 1985).<ref name="Greuter1985">{{cite journal |last1=Greuter |first1=W. |editor1-last=Greuter |editor1-first=W. |editor2-last=Raus |editor2-first=Th. |title=Dipsacaceae |journal=Willdenowia |date=1985 |volume=15 |pages=61–84 |series=Med-Checklist Notulae, 11.}}</ref> López González in 1987 agreed with the split.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=López González |first1=G. |title=Pterocephalidium, un nuevo género ibérico de la familia Dipsacaceae. |journal=43 |date=1987 |volume=Anales Jard. Bot. Madrid |pages=245–252}}</ref>

Greuter and Raus in 1985<ref name="Greuter1985" /> divided Iranian species of ''Scabiosa'' into two genera as ''Lomelosia'' (= ''Scabiosa'' sect. ''Astrocephalus'' and sect. ''Olivierinae'') and ''Scabiosa''  s. str. (''Scabiosa'' s. l sect. ''scaboisa'').<ref name="Ebadi">{{cite journal |last1=Ebadi |first1=Mostafa |last2=Siahkolaee |first2=Sedigheh Nikzat |title=Palynological study of some Iranian species of ''Scabiosa'' L. (Caprifoliaceae) |journal=Bangladesh Journal of Plant Taxonomy |date=December 2016 |volume=23 |issue=2 |page=215 |doi=10.3329/bjpt.v23i2.30855|doi-access=free }}</ref> ''Asterocephalus'' was later classed as a synonym of ''Scabiosa''.<ref>{{cite web |title=''Asterocephalus'' Zinn {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:14294-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |access-date=22 October 2023 |language=en}}</ref>

Soják in 1987 also transferred several species of Scabiosa taxa found in Asia, to the ''Lomelosia'' genus. Including ''Lomelosia flavida'' {{au|(Boiss. & Hausskn.) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia gumbetica'' {{au|(Boiss.) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia isetensis'' {{au|(L.) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia leucactis'' {{au|(Patzak) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia macrochaete'' {{au|(Boiss. & Hausskn.) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia olgae'' {{au|(Albov) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia rhodantha'' {{au|(Kar. & Kir.) Soják}}, ''Lomelosia speciosa'' {{au|(Royle) Soják}},, ''Lomelosia songarica'' {{au|(Schrenk) Soják}} and ''Lomelosia ucranica'' {{au|(L.) Soják}}.<ref>Soják, Sborn. Nár. Mus. v Praze, Řada B, Přír. Vĕdy 43 (1): 58 (1987)</ref>

There are 22 species of ''[[Scabiosa]]'' recorded in the ''[[Flora Iranica]]'' by [[Karl Heinz Rechinger|Rechinger]] in 1989, as he had not accepted the new term ''Lomelosia'' and had stated that species were later divided into two subgenera (''Scabiosa'' and ''Asterocephalus'') and three sections including ''Scabiosa'', ''Asterocephalus'' and ''Olivierianae''. He had used ''Scabiosa'' sect. ''Olivierianae'' to hold some of the annual species of ''Scabiosa'' subg. ''Asterocephalus''.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rechinger |first1=K.H. |title=''Scabioas'' sect. ''Olivierianae'', sect. nove (Dipsacaceae) |journal=Willdenowia |date=1989 |volume=19 |pages=137–151}}</ref>
New species from Iran and Asia were also added to these three sections.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Rechinger |first1=K.H. |last2=Lack |first2=H.W. |title=Flora Iranica. Flora des iranischen Hochlandes und der umrahmenden Gebirge. |volume=168 |publisher=Akademische Druck-und Verlagsanstalt |location=Graz |date=Jan 1991 |pages=1–67 |chapter=Dipsacaceae.}}</ref>

Molecular data demonstrated that ''Lomelosia'' (and ''Pycnocomon'') form a clade distinct from ''Scabiosa'' (and ''Sixalix'') (De Castro & Caputo 1997-1998,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=De Castro |first1=O. |last2=Caputo |first2=P. |title=A molecular reappraisal of ''Scabiosa'' L. and allied genera (Dipsacaceae). |journal=Delpinoa |date=1997–1998 |volume=39-40 |pages=99–108}}</ref>
Further [[Carpology|carpological]] (seed studies) and [[palynological]] studies by Mayer & Ehrendorf in 1999,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Mayer |first1=V. |last2=Ehrendorfer |first2=F. |title=Fruit differentiation, palynology, and systematics in the ''Scabiosa'' group of genera and ''Pseudoscabiosa'' (Dipsacaceae). |journal=Pl. Syst. Evol. |date=1999 |volume=216 |issue=1–2 |pages=135–166|doi=10.1007/BF00985103 |bibcode=1999PSyEv.216..135M }}</ref> have confirmed that ''Lomelosia'' was a separate genus from ''Scabiosa'', which has been further substantiated by later data from molecular [[phylogenetics]] (Caputo & al. 2004;<ref name=Caputo2004/> and Avino & al. 2009.<ref name="Avino & al. 2009"/> In 2009, the [[DNA]] clade of ''Scabioseae'', consisting of ''Pterocephalus'' s.str., ''Sixalix'', ''Scabiosa'', ''Lomelosia'', and ''Pycnocomon'' was established.<ref name="Carlson2009"/> ''Lomelosia'' can be distinguished from the other related genera by the presence of eight pits on the epicalyx tube (De Castro & Caputo 2001).<ref name="DeCastro2001">{{cite journal |last1=De Castro |first1=O. |last2=Caputo |first2=P. |title=A phylogenetic analysis of genus ''Lomelosia'' Rafin. (Dipsacaceae) and allied taxa. |journal=Delpinoa |orig-date=1999 |date=2000 |volume=41 |pages=29–45}}</ref>

The annual ''Lomelosia brachiata'' was resolved to be a ''genetic sister'' to the rest of ''Lomelosia'' and ''Pycnocomon'' in 2009.<ref name="Carlson2009"/>

In 2010, the Dipsacoideae subfamily was divided into 2 genetic DNA clades: the ''Knautia''–''Cephalaria''–''Dipsacus''–''Succisella'' clade and the ''Scabiosa''–''Pterocephalus''–''Lomelosia'' clade.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Temsch |first1=Eva M. |last2=Greilhuber |first2=Johann |title=Genome size in Dipsacaceae and Morina longifolia (Morinaceae) |journal=Plant Systematics and Evolution |date=2010 |volume=289 |issue=1–2 |pages=45–56|doi=10.1007/s00606-010-0330-1 |bibcode=2010PSyEv.289...45T }}</ref>

In 2011, 4 more species from the ''Scabiosa'' genus with a pitted epicalyx transferred to ''Lomelosia'', ''Lomelosia deserticola'' {{au|(Rech. f.) P. Caputo & Del Guacchio}}, ''Lomelosia poecilocarpa'' {{au|(Rech. f.) P. Caputo & Del Guacchio}}, ''Lomelosia schimperiana'' {{au|(Boiss. & Buhse) P. Caputo & Del Guacchio}}, and ''Lomelosia transcaspica'' {{au|(Rech. f.) P. Caputo & Del Guacchio}}.<ref name="Caputo2011"/>

In 2013, V. Mayer and Ehrend established ''Lomelosieae'', a subgenus within ''Lomelosia''.<ref name=Kadereit/>

==Description==
[[File:Lomelosia cretica RF.jpg|thumb|right|''Lomelosia cretica'' from Taormina, [[Sicily]] ]]
''Lomelosia'' is made up of perennial and annual, herbaceous species, examples include; ''Lomelosia persica'' is an annual species,<ref name=Kadereit/> while ''Lomelosia caucasica'' {{au|M. Bieb.}} is a perennial.

They are sometimes suffruticose (having a woody base, but herbaceous higher up), unarmed, without prickles. Leafy or scapiform stems, which have short hairs. It has [[Sessility (botany)|sessile]] or [[Petiole (botany)|petiolate]] (stalked) leaves. They are whole and toothed. They can be pinnatifid, pinnatipartite or [[Glossary of leaf morphology#pinnatisect|pinnatisect]]. The basal leaves are sometimes in a rosette, persistent or not at [[anthesis]] (fully open flowering). They have hemispheric chapters at anthesis which are globose or ovoid at the time of fruiting. It has numerous free bracts, arranged in 1-2 (or 3) rows. They are entire or pinnatifid, receptacle at first hemispherical and then conical or cylindrical (in shape), hairy, with herbaceous-scarious [[bracteoles]]. They have [[hermaphroditic]], [[Pentamerous (botany)|pentamerous]] (5 part) flowers, which are subactinomorphic (less than star-shaped) or [[Floral symmetry#Zygomorphic|zygomorphic]] (bilateral). They are almost equal or the peripheral ones of the larger chapter. The secondary involucre is homomorphous, sessile, without basal elasome, shaped like a cylindrical tube, with no apparent nerves or ribs and is densely hirsute. They have 8 foveolae in the distal half. It has a campanulate (bell-shaped) crown, which is scarious (dry and membranous) and hyaline (translucent), with ± scalloped edge. They are traversed by numerous smooth or scabrid nerves, sometimes excurrent (extending). The midriff is scarious and hairy. It consists of 8 [[Adnation|adnate]] tabs and with a cylindrical projection that surrounds the calyx stipe. The [[Sepal|calyx]] is pateniform, pentagonal (in shape), ± stipitate, with glanduliferous or eglandulous hairs. It has 5 subulate or widened edges at the base, they are ± scabrid, erect-patent or patent and persistent. The corolla has 5 lobes, that of the peripheral flowers are often zygomorphic, with very unequal lobes. As 3 are bigger than the other 2 lobes. The central is major, so that of the flowers in the center are subactinomorphic. They come in shades of pink, violet-pink or blue-like. The [[androecium]] is made of 4 stamens. The [[Stigma (botany)|stigma]] is capitate (resembles the head of a pin), bilobate (split in two) and oblique. The fruit (or seed capsule) is an [[achene]] (dry fruit) which is glabrous or hairy. (Translated from Spanish).<ref name=Devesa2007>{{cite book |last1=Devesa Alcaraz |first1=Juan Antonio |editor1-last=Alcaraz |editor1-first=Juan Antonio Devesa |editor2-last=Gonzalo |editor2-first=Raúl |editor3-last=Herrero |editor3-first=Alberto |title=Flora Iberica. Plantas vasculares de la Península Ibérica a Islas Baleares Vol.15 |date=January 2007 |publisher=Servicio de Publicaciones del CSIC |pages=336–347 |edition=1 |chapter=''Lomelosia''}}</ref>

They are generally have involucral bracts not connate (fused to another organ), entire and radiant, the epicalyx is cylindrical and the corona can be large.<ref name=Kadereit/>

