==Distribution==
''Lomelosia'' is described as an [[Old World]] genus and it is distributed from the Mediterranean region to Central Asia.<ref name=Belgium/><ref name=Ranjbar2018/>

It is found in the European countries (and regions) of Albania, the Balearic Islands, Bulgaria, Cyprus, East Aegean Islands, France, Greece, Italy,<ref name=Fabio/> Crete,<ref>John Fielding {{google books|RSslAQAAMAAJ|Flowers of Crete (2005)|page=199}}</ref> Portugal, Romania, Sardinia, Sicily, Spain, Switzerland, Ukraine and Yugoslavia.
In Russia, (Altai (region), Crimea, East European Russia, South European Russia and West Siberia.
In Northern Africa within Algeria, Egypt,<ref>Loutfy Boulos {{google books|Ae0lAQAAMAAJ|Flora of Egypt: Verbenaceae - Compositae (2005)|page=125}}</ref> Tunisia, Libya and Morocco.
In the Middle East,<ref>Ahmad K. Hegazy and Jon Lovett Doust {{google books|T_hGCwAAQBAJ|Plant Ecology in the Middle East (2016)|page=297}}</ref> within Afghanistan, Iran,<ref name=Ranjbar2018/> Iraq, Kuwait, Lebanon, Syria, Oman, Pakistan, Palestine, Saudi Arabia,<ref>{{cite web |title=Flora of Saudi Arabia- checklist |url=https://plantdiversityofsaudiarabia.info/Biodiversity-Saudi-Arabia/Flora/Checklist/Cheklist.htm |website=plantdiversityofsaudiarabia.info |access-date=21 October 2023}}</ref> and Turkey.
In central Asia within Kazakhstan, Kirghistan, North Caucasus, Tajikistan, Transcaucasus, Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan and West Himalaya.
Also in eastern Asia in [[Xinjiang]] (China).<ref name="Powo"/>

==Habitat==
[[File:Lomelosia graminifolia 2 RF.jpg|thumb|right|Grass-leaved scabious (''Lomelosia graminifolia'') near Limone sul Garda, Italy]]
''Lomelosia'' species like a range of habitats.

For example, ''Lomelosia cyprica'' is found on dry slopes in [[garigue]] (low scrubland) and open pine forests at altitudes of {{convert|150|and|1200|m|abbr=on}} above sea level.<ref>Alan Crivellaro and Fritz Hans Schweingruber {{google books|7PvFBAAAQBAJ|Atlas of Wood, Bark and Pith Anatomy of Eastern Mediterranean Trees and ...(2013)|page=258}}</ref>

While ''L.&nbsp;argentea'' likes arid meadows and garigue, ''L.&nbsp;crenata'' likes [[Detritus (geology)|detrital]] (rocky) slopes and ''L.&nbsp;graminifolia'' likes stony slopes (in Italy).<ref name=Fabio>Fabio Conti and Fabrizio Bartolucci {{google books|wDWSBgAAQBAJ|The Vascular Flora of the National Park of Abruzzo, Lazio and Molise (Central Italy):An annotated checklist (2015)|page=63}}</ref>

''Lomelosia minoana'' is Greek and Crete native rock-dwelling plant.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Grigoriadou |first1=Katerina |last2=Krigas |first2=Nikos |last3=Sarropoulou |first3=Virginia |last4=Maloupa |first4=Eleni |last5=Tsoktouridis |first5=Georgios |title=Propagation and ex-situ conservation of ''Lomelosia minoana'' subsp. ''minoana'' and ''Scutellaria hirta'' - two ornamental and medicinal Cretan endemics (Greece) |journal=Notulae Botanicae Horti Agrobotanici Cluj-Napoca |date=2021 |volume=49 |issue=1 |pages=1–13 |doi=10.15835/nbha[12168]|doi-broken-date=12 July 2025 }}</ref>

While ''Lomelosia deserticola'' {{au|(Rech.f.) P.Caputo & Del Guacchio}} is found in deserts of Syria, Jordan, Iraq and Pakistan and ''Lomelosia schimperiana'' {{au|(Boiss. & Buhse) P. Caputo & Del Guacchio}} is found in open environments in Iran.<ref name="Caputo2011"/>

==Ecology==
The flowers of ''Lomelosia'' are insect pollinated, mainly by bees and bumblebees, as the plants give them a reward of nectar and pollen.<ref name=Kadereit/> Other insects are occasional visitors, such as long-tongues hoverflies ([[Syrphidae]]),<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Kugler |first1=H. |title=Zum Problem der Dipterenblumen |journal=Österreichische Botanische Zeitschrift |date=1955 |volume=102 |issue=4–5 |pages=529–541 |doi=10.1007/BF02135228 |bibcode=1955PSyEv.102..529K |language=De}}</ref> moths (Proctor et al. 1996),<ref>{{cite book |last1=Proctor |first1=M. |last2=Yeo |first2=P. |last3=Lack |first3=A. |title=The natural history of pollination |date=1996 |publisher=Timber Press |location=Portland, Oregon, USA}}</ref> [[Lepidoptera]] (such as [[Lycaenidae]]) and [[Diptera]]) (Muller 1873,<ref>{{cite book |last1=Müller |first1=H. |title=Die Befruchtung der Blumen durch Insekten und die gegenseitigen Anpassungen beider. |date=1873 |publisher=W. Engelmann |location=Leipzig, Germany.}}</ref>).<ref name=Kadereit/>

==Uses==
''Lomelosia hymettia'' is considered as a medicinal plant (Grigoriadou et al., 2019),<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Grigoriadou |first1=K. |last2=Krigas |first2=Ν. |last3=Sarropoulou |first3=V. |last4=Papanastasi |first4=K. |last5=Tsoktouridis |first5=G. |last6=Maloupa |first6=E. |title=In-vitro propagation of medicinal and aromatic plants: the case of selected Greek species with conservation priority. |journal=Vitro Cellular & Developmental Biology |date=2019 |volume=55 |issue=6 |pages=635–646 |doi=10.1007/s11627-019-10014-6}}</ref> with [[antimicrobial]] properties (Christopoulou et al., 2008).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Christopoulou |first1=C. |last2=Graikou |first2=K. |last3=Chinou |first3=I. |title=Chemosystematic value of chemical constituents from ''Scabiosa hymettia'' (Dipsacaceae). |journal=Chemistry & Biodiversity |date=2008 |volume=5 |issue=2 |pages=318–323 |doi=10.1002/cbdv.200890029|pmid=18293445 }}</ref>

Several species of ''Lomelosia'' are grown as ornamentals in Europe. Jäger & al. (2008; sub ''Scabiosa'') cites ''L.&nbsp;caucasica'' {{au|(M. Bieb.) Greuter & Burdet}}, ''L.&nbsp;graminifolia'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}}, ''L.&nbsp;prolifera'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}} and '''L.&nbsp;stellata'' {{au|(L.) Raf}}. Matthews (2000; also sub Scabiosa) even cites 12 additional species that are cultivated in European gardens. One of these, ''L.&nbsp;stellata'', has been recorded as a ''casual escape'' in Belgium.<ref name=Belgium/>

While ''L.&nbsp;graminifolia'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}} (Barth, 2020),<ref>{{cite web |last1=Barth |first1=J.G. |title=Limestone and calcium in plants. Soin de la Terre  |url=http://www.soin-de-la-terre.org |access-date=11 May 2020 |date=2020}}</ref> ''L.&nbsp;hymettia'' {{au|(Boiss. & Spruner) Greuter & Burdet}} and ''L.&nbsp;cretica'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}} are [[xerophytes]] used in landscaping designs.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Papafotiou |first1=M. |last2=Kanellou |first2=E. |last3=Economou |first3=G. |title=Integrated design and management of vegetation at archaeological sites to protect monuments and enhance the historical landscape. |journal=Acta Horticulturae |date=2017 |volume=1189 |issue=1189 |pages=1–10 |doi=10.17660/ActaHortic.2017.1189.1}}</ref> ''L.&nbsp;prolifera'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}} has been used as part of [[Green roof|green roof plantations]] (Cristaudo et al., 2015).<ref>{{cite book |last1=Cristaudo |first1=A. |last2=Catara |first2=S. |last3=Impelluso |first3=C. |editor1-last=Mariotti |editor1-first=M. |editor2-last=Magrini |editor2-first=S. |title=Conservation of threatened species: activities and collaborations within the network. RIBES Series 1 |date=2015 |publisher=RIBES (Rete Italiana delle Banche per la conservazione Ex-Situ del germoplasma) |isbn=978-88-940844-0-5 |pages=53–56 |chapter=Conservation of native germplasm of the Mediterranean Region: ecological characterization of Sicilian flora species for sustainable use.}}</ref>

==Cultivation==
Successful [[Plant propagation|propagation]] in various species of ''Lomelosia'' has been reported both via seed growing and by vegetative methods.  Propagation with cuttings for species ''Lomelosia hymettia'' was carried out using [[Indole-3-butyric acid]] (IBA, hormone rooting agent) with results to a high rate of rooting (92.5–100%) during  autumn, compared to 50–67.5% during the spring  (Vlachou et  al.,  2019).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Vlachou |first1=G. |last2=Papafotiou |first2=M. |last3=Akoumianaki |first3=A. |last4=Bertsouklis |first4=K.F. |title=Propagation of Scabiosa hymettia (Boiss. & Spruner) by stem cuttings. |journal=Acta Horticulturae |date=2019 |volume=1242 |issue=1242 |pages=763–766 |doi=10.17660/ActaHortic.2019.1242.113}}</ref> In-vitro propagation has also been successfully employed for ''Lomelosia argentea'' {{au|(L.) Greuter & Burdet}} (Panayotova et al., 2008).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Panayotova |first1=L.G. |last2=Ivanova |first2=T.A. |last3=Bogdanova |first3=Y.Y. |last4=Gussev |first4=C.V. |last5=Stanilova |first5=M.I. |last6=Bosseva |first6=Y.Z. |last7=Stoeva |first7=T.D. |title=In-vitro cultivation of plant species from sandy dunes along the Bulgarian Black Sea Coast. |journal=Phytologia Balcanica |date=2008 |volume=14 |issue=1 |pages=119–123}}</ref>

